// PACKAGE-BLIND OPERATOR CALLEE. A struct `==` that reaches mono is rewritten
// by mono_clone (BinOp arm) into a call to `<pkg>.<Bare>__eq` — invented, with
// no check that the method exists. mlir-gen's find_func_op then resolved it
// through ffo_canonical, which STRIPS the package off callee and def alike, so
// `test.Ident__eq` bound
// `logos.std.compiler.metaprog.Ident__eq__f__ref_Ident__ref_Ident` and was
// handed two `%test.Ident`s: MEASURED SIGSEGV.
// ⚠ THE SHARPEST PART, and why this is a `fail` fixture and not a `pass` one:
// the collision-free twin (`_ctl`, byte-identical but for the name) does NOT
// compile either — `Ident` has no `eq`. The homonym program was accepted ONLY
// because it silently stole a foreign package's method. What is pinned here is
// that the two now behave IDENTICALLY: both refused, neither miscompiled.
// The `use` is load-bearing — it is what pulls logos.std.compiler.metaprog
// (and its own `Ident`) into the compile at all.
package test;
use logos.std.fmt;
struct Ident { pub k: i64, pub j: i64 }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a = Ident{k:1i64, j:2i64};
    let b = Ident{k:1i64, j:2i64};
    if a == b { return 7i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
